<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
    Arctic under-ice site for the BlueROV2 Heavy, used by UnderIceTest.

    A 200 x 200 m box of drifting first-year pack ice north of Svalbard, in about 48 m of
    water, cut by one open lead. The vehicle itself is defined in bluerov2_heavy.scn and
    included unchanged at the bottom of this file; the under-ice sensor payload is added
    from C++ in UnderIceTestManager (see WHY THE PAYLOAD IS NOT HERE, below).

    Frame convention is NED: X forward/north, Y starboard/east, Z DOWN. The water surface is
    z = 0, so ice draft is POSITIVE z and the ice canopy is an ordinary submerged static body.

    ================================ THE SITE ================================
        x in [-100, +100]        200 m
        y in [-100,  -6]         southern floe      IceFloeSouth  + IceEdgeSouth
        y in [  -6,  +6]         open lead, 12 m    (no ice: sky, daylight, and the only
                                                     place the vehicle can surface)
        y in [  +6, +100]        northern floe      IceFloeNorth  + IceEdgeNorth
        z = 45 .. 50             seabed             Seabed

        vehicle spawn            (-70, -12, 3)      under level ice of 1.7 m draft, 6 m south
                                                    of the lead edge, 45 m of water below

    Five pressure ridges cross the site at various angles. Running straight ahead from the
    spawn on the initial heading, along y = -12, the transect is:

        x = -70 .. -25      level ice, 1.5-1.8 m draft
        x = -20 ..   0      a ridge, keel 5-7 m           (50-70 m out)
        x =  10 ..  25      level ice again
        x =  40 ..  55      the big one, keel 10-12.7 m   (110-125 m out)

    so the forward sonar has something to find twice over, and the second time it matters.
    The deepest keel in the southern floe is 13.8 m, at (x = -12, y = -59); the northern floe
    reaches 14.2 m at (x = 81, y = 16). Away from the ridges the draft runs 1.5 m over level
    ice, 0.3 m over refrozen nilas and about 3 m over rafted patches.

    The vehicle starts UNDER the canopy rather than under the lead. It is +2.0 N buoyant and
    has no depth hold, so from a standing start in open water it simply surfaces. The lead is
    6 m to port - hold ',' to slide out into it and watch the upward sounder lose its return.
    UnderIceTest flies a 2 m standoff below the canopy off that sounder, which is what keeps
    the vehicle off the ice and the ice readouts alive; press Z to release it and the vehicle
    goes back to drifting up against the canopy, as it should.

    ============================ THE ICE, AND HOW IT IS BUILT ============================
    Both floes are `<static type="terrain">` - a Bullet btHeightfieldTerrainShape - ROTATED
    180 DEGREES ABOUT X so they hang as a ceiling instead of lying as a floor.

    A height field is the physically right representation here: ice draft genuinely is a
    single-valued function of position. The price is that overhangs and rafted undercuts
    cannot be expressed at all. No sensor in this scene resolves a re-entrant surface, so
    that is a fair trade; if you ever need one, add a small `<static type="model">` block on
    top of the terrain rather than trying to make the height field do it.

    Terrain also earns its place on cost. Terrain::getAABB returns an inverted box, so a
    200 m canopy is excluded from getWorldAABB and does not blow out the sun shadow
    cascades - a `type="model"` mesh of the same size would. And Bullet has a dedicated grid
    raycast accelerator for height fields (btCollisionWorld.cpp:416), which matters because
    the upward-looking multibeam fires 128 rays 5 times a second.

    THE 180 DEGREE FLIP IS NOT OPTIONAL. OpenGLContent::BuildTerrain seeds vertex normals at
    (0,0,-1) and winds its faces to match, i.e. facing the sky. The pipeline runs with
    glCullFace(GL_BACK), so an unflipped ice sheet would be culled away when viewed from
    below - invisible to the pilot camera AND to every rendered sonar, while still colliding
    perfectly. Rx(pi) has determinant +1, so the flip carries winding and normals with it and
    nothing else has to change.

    Heightmap encoding, worked through in Tests/Data/tools/make_sea_ice.py. Terrain.cpp
    INVERTS the image, and AddToSimulation composes its recentring offset on the RIGHT of the
    body transform, so it lands in the rotated frame:

        world_z = origin.z + maxHeight - heightfield,  heightfield = (1 - px/65535) * height
                = origin.z + (px/65535) * height       [given maxHeight == height]

    so BLACK = shallowest, WHITE = deepest keel, the opposite of the seabed convention. Each
    floe is encoded against its own draft extrema, which guarantees the pure-black pixel that
    makes maxHeight == height and spends all 16 bits on the range that floe covers. THE TWO
    FLOES THEREFORE DO NOT SHARE `height`. Regenerating the assets reprints both blocks; paste
    them, do not hand-edit them.

    16-bit is not optional either: 8 bit over a 12 m range quantises to 5 cm and terraces
    visibly in the upward-looking sonar.

    Grid is 0.75 m, which is the main performance knob in the whole scene. OpenGLPipeline
    draws every object in every pass with no frustum culling, and there are five views to
    render - the main one, the pilot camera, the forward sonar, and the sun's shadow cascades.
    At 0.75 m the two floes are 133k triangles between them; `--resolution 0.5` triples that
    for visibly finer keels, `--resolution 1.0` halves it again. Keels are 15-40 m wide, so
    even 1 m resolves their shape; everything finer than the grid is carried by the normal
    map rather than by geometry. Regenerating at a different resolution reprints the
    `<dimensions>` lines below - the `height` values change slightly with the grid, because
    each floe is encoded against the extrema its own samples happen to hit.

    ================================ THE LEAD ================================
    An open lead is the one thing a single height field cannot express, so there are two
    terrains with a gap. It is what gives the scene daylight from above, a place to surface,
    and a hard boundary for the upward sonar to report as "no return".

    A height field ends at a knife edge with zero thickness, which looks wrong from below at
    close range. IceEdgeSouth/North are thin boxes tucked under the last 0.4 m of each floe
    to cap it, and they double as a vertical target for the forward sonar. They can be a
    single fixed depth only because make_sea_ice.py tapers the last 4 m of each floe back to
    the level-ice draft - which is also the physically ordinary case, since a lead opens
    along a crack and cracks run through level ice, not through ridges.

    The caps stop at z = 0 with no freeboard. Nothing in this scene may sit above z = 0: the
    underwater material shader computes depth = P.z - waterLevel and multiplies albedo by
    exp(-c * depth) (Library/shaders/materialU.frag:63-89), so a fragment above the surface
    gets a POSITIVE exponent and blows out to white. The ice sail and the 0.31 m of freeboard
    are real, and are deliberately not modelled.

    `<waves height="0.0"/>` follows from the same shader. With waves on, waterLevel is an FFT
    displacement that oscillates about 0, and ice sitting near z = 0 flickers between the two
    shading regimes. A flat surface is also what is physically right under a consolidated
    canopy, and it makes Ocean::GetDepth a pure point.z so physics and visuals agree.

    ========================= MATERIALS ARE ALSO SONAR REFLECTIVITY =========================
    OpenGLFLS/SSS/MSIS shade an echo as clamp(dot(N, toEye), 0, 1) * restitution
    (Library/shaders/sonarInput.frag). The material's RESTITUTION IS ITS ACOUSTIC
    REFLECTIVITY, so it is derived here rather than picked for feel:

        R = (Z_ice - Z_water) / (Z_ice + Z_water)
        Z_ice   = 917  * 3840 = 3.52e6 Pa s/m       (sea ice, c ~ 3840 m/s)
        Z_water = 1027 * 1450 = 1.49e6 Pa s/m
        R = 0.405   ->  restitution 0.40

    Seabed silt at 2000 * 1800 = 3.6e6 gives 0.41; it is set to 0.45 so the bottom reads a
    little brighter than the ice on the sonar. The catch is that restitution is doing double
    duty - 0.40 is on the lively side for a mechanical contact, so the vehicle bounces off a
    keel slightly more than a real one would. Acoustics won, because the collision is a rare
    event and the sonar picture is on screen the whole time.

    Ice-on-hull friction is low on purpose: wet ice runs about 0.02-0.1. The vehicle is
    +2.0 N buoyant, so left alone it rises, pins itself against the canopy and slides along
    it. That is correct behaviour, not a bug.

    ========================= WHY THE PAYLOAD IS NOT HERE =========================
    `<sensor>` is only legal inside `<robot>`, and ScenarioParser::IncludeFiles only expands
    `<include>` elements that are DIRECT CHILDREN of `<scenario>` - it never recurses into a
    robot. So there is no way to add sensors to an included vehicle from the parent file. The
    alternatives were to duplicate all 515 lines of bluerov2_heavy.scn, or to push an FLS onto
    the shared vehicle and make the open-water demo pay for rendering it. Instead
    UnderIceTestManager::AddIcePayload() attaches them in C++ after parsing: the upward
    altimeter, the upward ice-draft multibeam, the forward FLS and an up-pointing light. There
    is no second camera - UnderIceTest tilts the bow camera instead, because a third rendered
    view is one too many for the render pipeline's view queue.

    ============================== KNOWN LIMITATIONS ==============================
    - No light is transmitted through the ice. Stonefish has no translucency, so under the
      canopy the only light is what the sun puts into the water column and what the vehicle's
      own lights provide. Sun elevation is the lever: it is set low here both because that is
      right for the latitude and season and because it keeps the under-ice volume dark.
    - No under-ice caustics, no refraction through the ice, no bubbles trapped against it.
    - BuildTerrain normalises UVs per axis (uv = (j/(nx-1), i/(ny-1)) * uvScale), so the
      texture is stretched by the terrain's aspect ratio. These floes are 200 x 94 m, so the
      ice texture is 2.1 times coarser along x than along y. Unavoidable without changing the
      library; barely visible on an organic texture.
    - The floes are fixed. Real pack ice drifts, deforms and closes leads. `<animated>` with a
      trajectory could drift them, but the whole canopy would have to move as one rigid piece.
      The 0.04 m/s current here stands in for the relative motion.
    - GPS is on the vehicle and is meaningless under a canopy. It is left in place because it
      belongs to the shared vehicle definition, and because watching it drop out under the
      ice and come back in the lead is itself instructive.
-->
<scenario>
	<solver>
		<multithreading max_physics_threads="8"/>
	</solver>

	<environment>
		<!-- North of Svalbard, on the drift. Late winter. -->
		<ned latitude="82.5" longitude="15.0"/>

		<ocean>
			<!-- Seawater at S = 34.5 sitting at its freezing point. Jerlov 0.05 is very clear
			     water, which is what winter under-ice conditions actually give: no bloom, no
			     river input, no wave-stirred sediment. -->
			<water density="1027.0" jerlov="0.05" temperature="-1.8"/>
			<waves height="0.0"/>
			<particles enabled="true"/>
			<current type="uniform">
				<velocity xyz="0.04 0.02 0.0"/>
			</current>
		</ocean>

		<atmosphere>
			<!-- Low sun: right for 82.5 N in late winter, and it keeps the water under the
			     canopy dark, which is the only way to fake the fact that no light gets
			     through the ice. Drop the elevation further if the scene still reads bright. -->
			<sun azimuth="180.0" elevation="6.0"/>
			<conditions temperature="-22.0" pressure="101300.0" humidity="0.75"/>
		</atmosphere>
	</environment>

	<materials>
		<!-- "Neutral" is required by bluerov2_heavy.scn. Physical materials can only be
		     defined in the main file, never in an included one. -->
		<material name="Neutral" density="1000.0" restitution="0.5"/>
		<material name="SeaIce" density="917.0" restitution="0.40"/>
		<material name="Seabed" density="2000.0" restitution="0.45"/>
		<friction_table>
			<friction material1="Neutral" material2="Neutral" static="0.5" dynamic="0.2"/>
			<friction material1="Neutral" material2="SeaIce" static="0.05" dynamic="0.02"/>
			<friction material1="Neutral" material2="Seabed" static="0.4" dynamic="0.2"/>
			<friction material1="SeaIce" material2="SeaIce" static="0.05" dynamic="0.02"/>
			<friction material1="SeaIce" material2="Seabed" static="0.3" dynamic="0.15"/>
			<friction material1="Seabed" material2="Seabed" static="0.9" dynamic="0.7"/>
		</friction_table>
	</materials>

	<looks>
		<!-- gray="1.0" so the albedo texture carries the colour undiluted, the same way
		     brov_hull does. The normal map is what supplies all relief finer than the 0.75 m
		     height field, and it also perturbs the normal the sonar shader uses, so the ice
		     speckles in the FLS image instead of returning a flat sheet. -->
		<look name="ice_underside" gray="1.0" roughness="0.35" metalness="0.0" reflectivity="0.03"
		      texture="ice/ice_albedo.png" normal_map="ice/ice_normal.png"/>
		<look name="ice_edge" gray="0.92" roughness="0.45" reflectivity="0.03"/>
		<look name="seabed" rgb="0.32 0.30 0.27" roughness="0.95"/>
	</looks>

	<!-- ==================== The ice canopy ====================
	     `height` and the world z come from make_sea_ice.py and differ per floe; see the
	     header. rpy="pi 0 0" is what turns a floor into a ceiling. -->
	<static name="IceFloeSouth" type="terrain">
		<height_map filename="ice/ice_draft_south_16b.png"/>
		<dimensions scalex="0.749064" scaley="0.752" height="13.4725"/>
		<material name="SeaIce"/>
		<look name="ice_underside" uv_scale="46.0"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 -53.00 0.3000" rpy="3.14159265 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<static name="IceFloeNorth" type="terrain">
		<height_map filename="ice/ice_draft_north_16b.png"/>
		<dimensions scalex="0.749064" scaley="0.752" height="13.8474"/>
		<material name="SeaIce"/>
		<look name="ice_underside" uv_scale="46.0"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 +53.00 0.3000" rpy="3.14159265 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<!-- Floe edges at the lead. 1.5 m deep to match the tapered level-ice margin, top at
	     z = 0 because nothing may sit above the surface. Tucked under the last 0.4 m of each
	     floe (y = -6.4..-6.0 and +6.0..+6.4), so the 12 m of open lead is not narrowed. -->
	<static name="IceEdgeSouth" type="box">
		<dimensions xyz="200.0 0.4 1.5"/>
		<material name="SeaIce"/>
		<look name="ice_edge" uv_mode="2"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 -6.2 0.75" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<static name="IceEdgeNorth" type="box">
		<dimensions xyz="200.0 0.4 1.5"/>
		<material name="SeaIce"/>
		<look name="ice_edge" uv_mode="2"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 +6.2 0.75" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<!-- ==================== Seabed ====================
	     Unflipped, so the usual seabed convention applies: the body spans world z in
	     [origin.z - maxHeight, origin.z], i.e. 45..50 m. It exists so the down-looking DVL has
	     something to bottom-lock on while the upward sensors work the ice.
	     The stock 32 x 32 heightmap, not the 256 x 256 one: at 6.5 m per pixel the relief is
	     coarse, but the bottom is 40 m below the vehicle in water this scene never lights, and
	     the finer map costs 130k triangles in every render pass - as much as both ice floes
	     together - to draw something nobody sees. -->
	<static name="Seabed" type="terrain">
		<height_map filename="terrain_small.png"/>
		<dimensions scalex="6.5" scaley="6.5" height="5.0"/>
		<material name="Seabed"/>
		<look name="seabed" uv_scale="24.0"/>
		<world_transform xyz="0.0 0.0 50.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
	</static>

	<!-- Spawns under the open lead on level ice margins, heading +x. The big keel is about
	     55 m dead ahead. -->
	<include file="bluerov2_heavy.scn">
		<arg name="robot_name" value="BLUEROV2"/>
		<arg name="robot_position" value="-70.0 -12.0 3.0"/>
		<arg name="dvl_up" value="1"/>
	</include>
</scenario>
